Halide Bloom
Ingredients: PLUGGNB + UPLIFTING / EPIC TRANCE + TRIPPY, SURREAL & PSYCHEDELIC + Synth + 1980s ERA
Creative Catalyst: Rogue cinema projectionists composed private anthems to soundtrack phantom reels and silent celluloid epics that never truly existed.

Halide Bloom is a towering, feverish collision of bedroom vulnerability and arena-scale transcendence. It takes the melancholic Rhodes voicings, tumbling hi-hats, and sliding 808s of pluggnb, and violently propels them into the stratospheric, 140-BPM emotional architecture of epic uplifting trance. Drenched in the hallucinatory shimmer of 1980s digital synthesizers, the sound feels both micro-intimate and impossibly colossal—like crying in the back row of an abandoned cinema while the screen detonates in blinding white light.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 138–142 BPM mapped in double-time over a sluggish 69–71 BPM half-time pulse. The genre completely abolishes the standard four-on-the-floor trance kick, replacing it with syncopated, stumbling 808 sub-bass slides, erratic rimshots, and airy triplet hi-hat flutters that hang weightless between supersaw swells.
- Harmonic Palette: Heartbreaking Lydian and Dorian modes; classic uplifting trance chord stacks reharmonized with rich, melancholic pluggnb jazz tensions (minor 9ths, suspended 4ths, add11ths) that balance tragic longing with divine euphoria.
- Production Signatures:
  1. Optical-Gate Flutter-Printing (Impossible/Evocative Technique): Stems are routed through an analog light sensor wired directly inside a running 1930s 35mm projector loaded with clear celluloid; the hot xenon bulb and the mechanical 24-fps shutter chop the audio into a physical, thermal-drifted 24Hz tremolo.
  2. Sub-Inversion Anti-Drops: 64-bar epic builds crescendo into blinding white noise and supersaws, but instead of a thumping kick drum, the climax drops into total silence punctured only by an ultra-deep, pitch-sliding 808 bass glide and a solitary FM electric piano chime.
  3. Acetate Phase Smear: 1980s digital synth leads are bounced onto baking-hot quarter-inch tape, flanged through dual vintage bucket-brigade delays, and pitch-warped to simulate melting film stock.
- Dynamics: Three-act cinematic arcs that swing radically from whisper-quiet confessional verses to stadium-sized sonic tidal waves; tracks breathe through long, drumless breakdowns that hover in psychedelic suspension before unleashing euphoric, tear-soaked climaxes.

Scene & Origin Lore
Halide Bloom emerged in the late 1990s and early 2000s within the crumbling single-screen movie palaces of post-industrial ports like Valparaíso, Genoa, and Detroit. Graveyard-shift projectionists, bored of running faded archival prints, began splicing together discarded leader tape, blank celluloid, and chemical-stained film strips to create "phantom reels"—movies with no actors, no plots, and no titles. Armed with cheap, neglected 1980s synthesizers (snatched from pawnshops) and early drum machines, they scored these silent optical hallucinations live from their projection booths, piping the sound through the cinema PA systems to small gatherings of insomniac artists. The dress code evolved directly from booth work: grease-splattered linen smocks, vintage optometrist lenses tinted deep amber to shield against xenon-lamp glare, and unspooled 35mm film strips worn as scarves or knotted belts. Their core ritual, the "Nitrate Vigil," involves playing unannounced all-night sets to blank, flickering screens until the sun burns through the stained-glass exit doors.

Central Instruments: Yamaha DX7 (FM glass keys and metallic tines), Roland D-50 (ethereal breath and choral patches), Roland Juno-106 (chorus-drenched supersaws), Roland TR-808 (heavily saturated, sliding bass), E-mu Emulator II (lo-fi orchestral sweeps), modified 35mm cinema projectors, Roland Space Echo RE-201, auto-tuned and pitch-shifted vocal vocoders.

For Fans Of: Evian Christ, Autumn!, Poliça, early Above & Beyond (Acoustic & Club hybrid eras).

